{
  "image_name": "3dbba4b117a12e1d50098e8c881d6d26.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares how oxygen ions and oxygen vacancies move through an oxide particle under opposite chemical conditions, illustrating the basic operating mechanism of a mixed ionic–electronic conductor. In both schematics, heat drives transport through the particle, and a shaded blue gradient indicates a difference in oxygen chemical potential or oxygen partial pressure from one side to the other.\n\nOn the left, hydrogen reacts with lattice oxygen at the surface to form water, leaving behind electrons and oxygen vacancies. These vacancies are then generated near the low-oxygen-potential side, diffuse through the bulk, and are linked to opposite electronic motion inside the material. On the right, oxygen from the gas phase is incorporated into a vacancy at the surface, producing oxide ions and holes; here the oxide ions and vacancies move in the reverse direction relative to the left panel.\n\nThe numbered steps summarize the same general sequence in both cases: creation of an oxygen ion or vacancy, bulk diffusion through the particle, and a surface reaction with the surrounding gas. Overall, the image shows how redox surface reactions, ionic defects, and electronic carriers couple to transport oxygen through a heated oxide.",
